[size=5][color=#B8860B][b]Vivid World — A Life of Their Own[/b][/color][/size]

A singleplayer mod for Mount & Blade II: Bannerlord. [b]It brings the people of Calradia to life.[/b]
Things happen between them, word gets around and changes on the way. They remember, and they forget.
And you can be part of it.

[color=#999999][i]This mod is currently in beta. The core systems are finished and running in real campaigns; content is still being added.[/i][/color]


[size=4][color=#B8860B][b]Things happen between them[/b][/color][/size]

All kinds of scenes play out among the lords on their own, such as a quarrel over seating at a feast or words said over too much wine. The same scene does not always go the same way — someone loses face, someone holds a grudge, and sometimes someone puts in a good word.

The game's own events feed in as well — death, capture, release, marriage, childbirth — told apart by cause and circumstance into different kinds of news.


[size=4][color=#B8860B][b]Word gets around, and changes on the way[/b][/color][/size]

Every so often each NPC picks something from memory and tells it to whoever is nearby. They say whether they saw it themselves or who told them; those involved speak of their own affairs in their own voice. Every retelling loses a little more: the day, the place, who else was there. The further it travels, the more likely what reaches the listener is no more than "someone did something somewhere."

Bigger news travels further. Word of a newborn does not get far; a murdered ruler can be talked about across half the continent. [color=#D9534F][b]Good news travels slowly. Bad news travels far.[/b][/color]


[size=4][color=#B8860B][b]They remember, and they forget[/b][/color][/size]

Someone with no ties to the parties involved holds onto it for a few days. Something brought up again and again is remembered longer. Once everyone who knew has forgotten, the matter goes quiet for good.


[size=4][color=#B8860B][b]What they hear changes how they stand with each other[/b][/color][/size]

Whoever hears the news thinks differently of the people in it, by as much as the event is worth — in full if they saw it themselves, less if they only heard about it, and less again with every retelling.

Bad feeling is written into the mod's own ledger of grudges. Let it run deep enough, and if the person involved is a clan leader or their close kin, a quarrel between two people becomes a feud between two clans. Grudges fade with time, but never all the way back to nothing.


[size=4][color=#B8860B][b]Not everything said is true[/b][/color][/size]

Lords will say things behind others' backs that never happened — smearing someone they bear a grudge against, running down a rival for position, making up good deeds for their own family. Whoever hears it decides whether to believe it: someone close to the person talked about, someone who distrusts the teller, or someone who finds it out of character will not change their view because of it. The person talked about may deny it publicly, and someone who was there and knows the truth may set it straight; those who heard it will think again.


[size=4][color=#B8860B][b]You can be part of it[/b][/color][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Ask.[/b] Talking to any lord or wanderer adds the line "Any news on the road?". Someone who barely knows you relates only the big news everyone is talking about; someone close to you starts with what touches him. When he has nothing for you, he tells you why. [color=#D9534F][b]Getting nothing out of a stranger is normal[/b][/color] — whether he talks depends on how he feels about you and on his character. That is by design.
[*][b]Be told.[/b] Lords and wanderers will sometimes tell you news without being asked. What they bring up is their own business and that of the people they care about; having told it, they add a line of their own — a friend, an enemy and a bystander tell the same event differently. Each person will only share so much with you in a day; once he has said enough, he will tell you to come back another time.
[*][b]Probe.[/b] In conversation, choose "There's something I want to ask you." and pick an item from "What you have heard" to ask about. They answer from what they know — never heard of it, the part they know, or, if they have heard both versions, which one they believe; ask about their own affairs and they may deny it. Each person will only let you ask so much in a day.
[*][b]Rumor mode.[/b] Choose Auto, Casual or Realistic in MCM: in Casual anyone whose character does not shy from talk may bring up his own affairs even to a new acquaintance, and commoners can ask too; in Realistic he must be talkative by nature, or think somewhat well of you. Auto (the default) uses Realistic when a mod such as NaN or Lowborn is detected, Casual otherwise.
[*][b]Personal relations.[/b] Everyone keeps their own opinion of you: helping or crossing someone only affects that person, not their whole clan (in the base game, a clan shares its leader's relation with you). Who is willing to talk to you, and what they think of you when news involves you, also follow that person's own opinion. The first time you load an existing campaign, each person gets the value shown on screen at that moment, so the numbers you see do not change. It can be turned off in MCM to go back to the original rule.
[*][b]Look back.[/b] Press [b]Ctrl+L[/b] for "What you have heard" — each matter that has reached you, follow-ups and differing accounts together, marked "Conflicting accounts" when they disagree, with each person's own words kept as a separate entry and how many tellings away it is. Names of people and places link to the encyclopedia.
[*][b]You get talked about too.[/b] What you do enters the same network and reaches places you have never been.
[/list]


[size=4][color=#B8860B][b]Installation[/b][/color][/size]

[b]Game version:[/b] built against [b]v1.4.8[/b]. Other versions are untested.

[list=1]
[*]Extract and place the whole VividWorld folder into the game's Modules\ directory.
[*][color=#D9534F][b]Install Mod Configuration Menu (MCM) as well[/b][/color], loaded before this mod.
[*]Tick [b]Vivid World[/b] in the launcher, after Native, SandBoxCore, Sandbox and StoryMode.
[*]Harmony does not need to be installed separately.
[/list]

[b]Uninstalling:[/b] delete the module folder. Back up your saves first.


[size=4][color=#B8860B][b]Save safety[/b][/color][/size]

The rumor data is [b]not written into your save files[/b]. It lives in
Documents\Mount and Blade II Bannerlord\Configs\VividWorld\

[list]
[*][b]Remove the mod and your existing saves still load.[/b] The personal relations recorded by "Personal relations" stay in the save, but the base game does not read them, and the screen goes back to the value a clan shares from its leader.
[*][b]Move a save to another machine and the rumours do not follow[/b] — copy that folder as well.
[/list]


[size=4][color=#B8860B][b]Compatibility[/b][/color][/size]

[b]Mods where commoners may not address nobles[/b] (NaN, Lowborn and the like): Vivid World detects them and, with the rumor mode on Auto, switches to Realistic, limiting asking for news to clan tier 1 and above. At tier 0 you will get nothing, and that is deliberate; to let a commoner ask anyway, switch to Casual.

[b]Calradia Remembers[/b]: Vivid World is currently integrated with Calradia Remembers only. With it installed, when you chat with a lord or wanderer through AI, he knows the rumors he has heard in Vivid World and still remembers — in the version he heard, and he can say who told him; what he has forgotten or what is long out of date does not come up. Other AI chat mods do not receive these rumors yet; without Calradia Remembers, nothing in Vivid World is affected. The language used for this is set in the "AI Integration" group in MCM (English by default).

[b]Mods that take over a lord's opening line:[/b] some mods claim the first thing a lord says. When that happens, an extra line appears in the dialogue menu — "You looked like you had something to say?" — to bring the subject back.

[b]Affairs of Calradia[/b]: with its personal relations mode on, it does the same thing as "Personal relations", and the two do not conflict; Vivid World detects that it is already being done and does not redistribute relations.

Apart from "Personal relations" changing how the game computes someone's relation with you, Vivid World does not change the game's own AI decisions and overwrites no native files.


[size=4][color=#B8860B][b]Settings[/b][/color][/size]

[b]There is a Vivid World page in the game's settings screen[/b] (provided by Mod Configuration Menu), all changeable in game.

The settings file lives at Documents\Mount and Blade II Bannerlord\Configs\VividWorld\config.json and is created the first time you run the game — the master switch, the hotkeys, how many snapshots to keep, and more. Both routes write the same file, and settings not on the page are left untouched.

[b]Updating the mod never changes settings you have altered[/b] — only missing keys are added.


[size=4][color=#B8860B][b]Languages[/b][/color][/size]

English and [b]Traditional Chinese[/b] are included. More languages are welcome.


[size=4][color=#B8860B][b]Roadmap[/b][/color][/size]

[list]
[*][b]More scenes of their own[/b] — more situations playing out between them: feasts, campaigns, markets, families.
[*][b]Setting the record straight[/b] — confront the person involved when what you heard does not match the truth.
[*][b]Conflicts with a reply[/b] — duels and other back-and-forth, so a quarrel is not settled in one stroke.
[*][b]Consequences that reach further[/b] — outcomes touching money, renown, influence, and personal safety.
[*][b]Your turn to talk[/b] — spread news yourself, set people against each other, make rumour a tool.
[*][b]More sources of news[/b] — defections, rebellions and fallen cities travelling among them too.
[*][b]Deeper ties with AI dialogue mods[/b] — beyond the rumors they have heard, making what they hold against each other available to mods that drive conversation with AI.
[/list]


[size=4][color=#B8860B][b]Reporting problems[/b][/color][/size]

The log is at Documents\Mount and Blade II Bannerlord\Configs\VividWorld\log.txt — please attach it. If the problem is about who told what to whom, set debug.logTellerTurns to true in config.json, restart, reproduce it, and the log will say a great deal more.


[size=4][color=#B8860B][b]Note[/b][/color][/size]

This is my first mod, and I use AI (Claude) to develop. Feel free to leave a comment or report a bug on Nexus, or if you would like to contribute to this mod, you can visit my [url=https://github.com/PH010546/VividWorld]github repo[/url].
